  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Flood Watch in effect for the entire area for \nstrong to severe showers and thunderstorms through tomorrow. \n\nThe latest surface analysis shows a warm front well northeast \nof the area, with a cold front stretching from the eastern Great\nLakes to IL and westward. This will be the main driving force \nfor severe weather later this evening. Aloft, troughing \nencompasses much of the eastern states along and north of the \nMid Atlantic region. \n\nThe first wave of positive vorticity advection was shifting into\nthe area and had sparked a line of storms moving through SE \nWV and into northern VA. These storms are mainly a flood threat\nif they continue to train. As they shift south and interact \nwith addition instability this afternoon and evening, expect a \nfew strong to severe storms. Along the cold front, more \nwidespread clusters of multicells push south after 4 to 5 pm \nand tonight. Wet microbursts will be the main concern today and \ntonight, although all convection may have torrential rainfall. \nShear between 0-6 km increases to 35 to 45 kts by later this \nafternoon and evening as the nose of the 300mb upper level jet \nreaches the area, along and north of the front. Also concerning \nwill be the increasing 0-1 km shear that may reach 25 kts, in \nthe vicinity of the front where surface winds may back. This \npoints towards increasing low level rotation resulting in a few \nsupercells and a small tornado risk.\n\nThe cold front looks to slow down as it crosses the forecast \narea from north to south, stalling somewhere in VA or northern \nNC overnight. Thus the high threat for urban, small stream, and \nflash flooding will continue overnight into tomorrow with \ntraining cells along the boundary. CAMs are also hinting at an \nMCV or two in the area tomorrow morning, which would support \nstronger and heavier storms. Additional development is expected \ntomorrow, with additional short wave energy and favorable\nkinematics moving into the Mid Atlantic region. Along the \nfront, the flooding threat will continue through into tomorrow \nnight. \n  \nAs drier air begins to work in from the north by Thursday,\nchances for rain sink south. After a brief break, more chances\nfor showers and storms return Friday into the weekend, as \nadditional mid level short waves move through. With the numerous\nchances for rain and cloud cover in the forecast, temperatures \nwill be in the 70s for the entire area Thursday through \nSaturday.",
